window.DD_FULL = window.DD_FULL || {}; window.DD_FULL["gate-1"] = {"questions":[{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Responding to a question about a seat","th":"การตอบคำถามเรื่องที่นั่งว่าง"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best response to complete the conversation.\nA: \"Excuse me, is this seat free?\"\nB: \"______\"","choices":["No, I'm not free today.","Yes, it's very expensive.","Sorry, my friend is sitting there.","You're welcome."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"Here \"free\" means \"not being used\". \"Sorry, my friend is sitting there.\" politely says the seat is taken. \"It's very expensive\" mixes up free (no cost) with free (empty), and \"I'm not free today\" talks about the speaker's time.","th":"คำว่า free ในที่นี้หมายถึง \"ว่าง ไม่มีคนนั่ง\" คำตอบ \"Sorry, my friend is sitting there.\" บอกอย่างสุภาพว่ามีคนนั่งแล้ว ส่วน \"It's very expensive\" สับสนกับความหมาย \"ฟรี ไม่เสียเงิน\" และ \"I'm not free today\" พูดถึงเวลาว่างของผู้พูด"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Accepting an apology","th":"การตอบรับคำขอโทษ"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best response to complete the conversation.\nA: \"I'm so sorry I'm late. The bus broke down.\"\nB: \"______ Come in and sit down.\"","choices":["Don't worry about it.","Help yourself.","It's my pleasure.","Same to you."],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"\"Don't worry about it.\" is the natural way to accept an apology. \"It's my pleasure.\" answers \"thank you\", \"Same to you.\" returns a wish, and \"Help yourself.\" offers food.","th":"\"Don't worry about it.\" (ไม่เป็นไร) ใช้ตอบรับคำขอโทษ ส่วน \"It's my pleasure.\" ใช้ตอบคำขอบคุณ \"Same to you.\" ใช้อวยพรกลับ และ \"Help yourself.\" ใช้เชิญให้หยิบอาหารเอง"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Refusing an offer politely","th":"การปฏิเสธข้อเสนออย่างสุภาพ"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best response to complete the conversation.\nA: \"Would you like some more rice?\"\nB: \"______ I'm really full.\"","choices":["Yes, I would.","Go ahead.","No, thanks.","Here you are."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"B is full, so B refuses: \"No, thanks.\" \"Yes, I would.\" accepts the rice, which does not match \"I'm really full\". \"Here you are.\" is said when giving something.","th":"B อิ่มแล้ว จึงต้องปฏิเสธว่า \"No, thanks.\" ส่วน \"Yes, I would.\" เป็นการรับ ซึ่งขัดกับ \"I'm really full\" และ \"Here you are.\" ใช้ตอนยื่นของให้"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Asking a follow-up question","th":"การถามคำถามต่อเนื่องให้สอดคล้องกับบทสนทนา"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best response to complete the conversation.\nA: \"I've lost my phone again!\"\nB: \"______\"\nA: \"Yes, I've looked in my bag, on my desk and even in the fridge!\"","choices":["How much did it cost?","Have you checked everywhere?","Can I borrow it?","Where did you buy it?"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"A's reply (\"Yes, I've looked in my bag, on my desk…\") answers a yes/no question about searching, so B must ask \"Have you checked everywhere?\" The other questions do not lead to that reply.","th":"คำตอบของ A (\"Yes, I've looked in my bag…\") ตอบคำถามว่าหาแล้วหรือยัง B จึงต้องถามว่า \"Have you checked everywhere?\" (หาทุกที่แล้วหรือยัง) คำถามอื่นไม่ได้นำไปสู่คำตอบนี้"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Giving an opinion","th":"การแสดงความคิดเห็น"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best response to complete the conversation.\nA: \"What do you think of the new school canteen?\"\nB: \"______ The food is much better than last year's.\"","choices":["It's up to you.","I don't think so.","I'm really impressed.","I'd rather not."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"B gives a positive opinion supported by \"The food is much better\". \"I'm really impressed.\" fits. \"I don't think so.\" answers a yes/no question, \"It's up to you.\" lets someone else decide, and \"I'd rather not.\" refuses an offer.","th":"B แสดงความเห็นในทางบวก และให้เหตุผลว่าอาหารดีขึ้นมาก \"I'm really impressed.\" (ประทับใจมาก) จึงเหมาะ ส่วน \"I don't think so.\" ใช้ตอบคำถามใช่/ไม่ใช่ \"It's up to you.\" ให้อีกฝ่ายตัดสินใจ และ \"I'd rather not.\" เป็นการปฏิเสธ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Declining an invitation and suggesting another time","th":"การปฏิเสธคำชวนและเสนอเวลาอื่น"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best response to complete the conversation.\nA: \"Shall we go to the museum on Saturday?\"\nB: \"______ I've got a swimming competition that day. How about Sunday?\"","choices":["I'm afraid I can't.","Why not?","I'd love to.","That's a great idea."],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"B has a competition on Saturday and suggests Sunday, so B must decline: \"I'm afraid I can't.\" \"That's a great idea.\", \"I'd love to.\" and \"Why not?\" all accept the invitation.","th":"B ติดแข่งว่ายน้ำวันเสาร์และเสนอวันอาทิตย์แทน จึงต้องปฏิเสธอย่างสุภาพว่า \"I'm afraid I can't.\" ส่วน \"That's a great idea.\", \"I'd love to.\" และ \"Why not?\" ล้วนเป็นการตอบรับคำชวน"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Encouraging someone after bad news","th":"การให้กำลังใจเมื่อได้รับข่าวร้าย"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best response to complete the conversation.\nA: \"I failed my maths quiz even though I studied all night.\"\nB: \"______ You'll do better next time.\"","choices":["Good for you!","Congratulations!","Well done!","Cheer up!"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"A has bad news, so B offers comfort: \"Cheer up!\" \"Well done!\", \"Congratulations!\" and \"Good for you!\" praise success and would sound rude here.","th":"A ได้รับข่าวไม่ดี B จึงควรปลอบใจว่า \"Cheer up!\" (ร่าเริงหน่อยนะ) ส่วน \"Well done!\", \"Congratulations!\" และ \"Good for you!\" ใช้ชมเมื่อประสบความสำเร็จ จึงไม่เหมาะ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Understanding an idiom in conversation","th":"การเข้าใจสำนวนในบทสนทนา"},"topic":{"en":"Idioms and expressions","th":"สำนวนและวลีในบทสนทนา"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best response to complete the conversation.\nA: \"Do you need any help with the science project?\"\nB: \"Thanks, but I've done most of it. The last part is ______. I'll be finished in ten minutes.\"","choices":["under the weather","once in a blue moon","a piece of cake","a fish out of water"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"\"A piece of cake\" means \"very easy\", which matches \"I'll be finished in ten minutes\". \"A fish out of water\" means uncomfortable in a new place, \"under the weather\" means slightly ill, and \"once in a blue moon\" means very rarely.","th":"\"a piece of cake\" แปลว่า ง่ายมาก สอดคล้องกับ \"อีกสิบนาทีก็เสร็จ\" ส่วน \"a fish out of water\" หมายถึงรู้สึกไม่คุ้นเคยกับสถานที่ \"under the weather\" หมายถึงไม่ค่อยสบาย และ \"once in a blue moon\" หมายถึงนาน ๆ ครั้ง"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Choosing an adjective that fits the context","th":"การเลือกคำคุณศัพท์ให้เข้ากับบริบท"},"topic":{"en":"Words in context","th":"คำศัพท์ตามบริบท"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"Engineers closed the old bridge because it was no longer ______ enough to carry heavy trucks safely.\"","choices":["fragile","sturdy","flexible","hollow"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"\"Sturdy\" means strong and solid. The bridge was closed because it was no longer strong enough. \"Fragile\" (easily broken) is the opposite meaning, and \"hollow\" and \"flexible\" do not describe the ability to carry heavy loads.","th":"\"sturdy\" แปลว่า แข็งแรง มั่นคง สะพานถูกปิดเพราะไม่แข็งแรงพอจะรับรถบรรทุก ส่วน \"fragile\" (เปราะ แตกง่าย) มีความหมายตรงข้าม \"hollow\" (กลวง) และ \"flexible\" (ยืดหยุ่น) ไม่ได้บอกความสามารถในการรับน้ำหนัก"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Choosing an everyday phrasal verb","th":"การเลือกกริยาวลีที่ใช้ในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"topic":{"en":"Phrasal verbs and collocations","th":"กริยาวลีและคำที่ใช้คู่กัน"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"Please ______ the lights when you leave the classroom to save electricity.\"","choices":["turn up","take off","turn off","put away"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"To save electricity we \"turn off\" the lights. \"Turn up\" means increase (volume or heat), \"take off\" is used for clothes or planes, and \"put away\" means return something to its place.","th":"การประหยัดไฟคือการปิดไฟ ใช้ \"turn off\" ส่วน \"turn up\" แปลว่า เร่ง/เพิ่ม (เสียง ความร้อน) \"take off\" ใช้กับการถอดเสื้อผ้าหรือเครื่องบินขึ้น และ \"put away\" แปลว่า เก็บเข้าที่"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Finding a synonym from context","th":"การหาคำที่มีความหมายใกล้เคียงจากบริบท"},"topic":{"en":"Words in context","th":"คำศัพท์ตามบริบท"},"passage":"","stem":"Which word is closest in meaning to \"reluctant\" in the sentence below?\n\"Even after the storm warning, the fishermen were reluctant to return to shore without a single catch.\"","choices":["prepared","unwilling","frightened","eager"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"\"Reluctant\" means not wanting to do something, so \"unwilling\" is closest. The fishermen did not want to go back with no fish. \"Eager\" is the opposite.","th":"\"reluctant\" แปลว่า ไม่เต็มใจ จึงใกล้เคียงกับ \"unwilling\" ชาวประมงไม่อยากกลับฝั่งมือเปล่า ส่วน \"eager\" (กระตือรือร้น) มีความหมายตรงข้าม"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Choosing the correct phrasal verb","th":"การเลือกกริยาวลีให้ถูกความหมาย"},"topic":{"en":"Phrasal verbs and collocations","th":"กริยาวลีและคำที่ใช้คู่กัน"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"The sports day has been ______ until next Friday because of the heavy rain forecast.\"","choices":["taken off","put off","put on","called up"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"\"Put off\" means postpone (move to a later time), which fits \"until next Friday\". \"Put on\" means organise a show or wear clothes, \"taken off\" is for clothes or planes, and \"called up\" means telephoned.","th":"\"put off\" แปลว่า เลื่อนออกไป เหมาะกับ \"until next Friday\" ส่วน \"put on\" แปลว่า จัดการแสดงหรือสวมใส่ \"taken off\" ใช้กับการถอดหรือเครื่องบินขึ้น และ \"called up\" แปลว่า โทรหา"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Choosing the correct word form (adjective)","th":"การเลือกรูปคำให้ถูกชนิด (คำคุณศัพท์)"},"topic":{"en":"Word formation","th":"การสร้างคำ"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"Our teacher gave us some very ______ advice about how to prepare for the exam.\"","choices":["usefully","usage","useful","use"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"Between \"very\" and the noun \"advice\" we need an adjective: \"useful\". \"Use\" and \"usage\" are nouns/verbs, and \"usefully\" is an adverb.","th":"ระหว่าง \"very\" กับคำนาม \"advice\" ต้องใช้คำคุณศัพท์ คือ \"useful\" ส่วน \"use\" และ \"usage\" เป็นคำนามหรือกริยา และ \"usefully\" เป็นคำกริยาวิเศษณ์"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Make or do collocations","th":"การใช้ make และ do ให้ถูกคู่คำ"},"topic":{"en":"Phrasal verbs and collocations","th":"กริยาวลีและคำที่ใช้คู่กัน"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"My little brother always ______ a lot of noise when he plays video games.\"","choices":["takes","gives","does","makes"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"In English we say \"make a noise\". \"Do\" goes with homework or exercise, \"take\" with a photo or a break, and \"give\" with advice or a speech.","th":"ภาษาอังกฤษใช้ \"make a noise\" (ส่งเสียงดัง) ส่วน \"do\" ใช้กับการบ้านหรือการออกกำลังกาย \"take\" ใช้กับถ่ายรูปหรือพัก และ \"give\" ใช้กับคำแนะนำหรือการกล่าวสุนทรพจน์"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Finding an antonym from context","th":"การหาคำตรงข้ามจากบริบท"},"topic":{"en":"Words in context","th":"คำศัพท์ตามบริบท"},"passage":"","stem":"Which word is OPPOSITE in meaning to \"ancient\" in the sentence below?\n\"We visited an ancient temple that was built more than 700 years ago.\"","choices":["ruined","modern","famous","huge"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"\"Ancient\" means very old, so its opposite is \"modern\" (new, of the present time). \"Huge\", \"famous\" and \"ruined\" could also describe a temple, but they are not opposites of \"old\".","th":"\"ancient\" แปลว่า เก่าแก่มาก คำตรงข้ามคือ \"modern\" (ทันสมัย) ส่วน \"huge\" (ใหญ่) \"famous\" (มีชื่อเสียง) และ \"ruined\" (ปรักหักพัง) ใช้บรรยายวัดได้ แต่ไม่ใช่คำตรงข้ามของ \"เก่าแก่\""}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Choosing precise academic vocabulary","th":"การเลือกคำศัพท์เชิงวิชาการให้แม่นยำ"},"topic":{"en":"Words in context","th":"คำศัพท์ตามบริบท"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"Her explanation of the experiment was so ______ that even the youngest students understood it at once.\"","choices":["obscure","tedious","lucid","vague"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"\"Lucid\" means very clear and easy to understand, which explains why even young students understood at once. \"Obscure\" and \"vague\" mean unclear, and \"tedious\" means boring.","th":"\"lucid\" แปลว่า ชัดเจน เข้าใจง่าย จึงทำให้แม้แต่เด็กเล็กที่สุดก็เข้าใจทันที ส่วน \"obscure\" และ \"vague\" แปลว่า คลุมเครือ ไม่ชัดเจน และ \"tedious\" แปลว่า น่าเบื่อ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Using context clues to choose an adjective","th":"การใช้คำใบ้จากบริบทเลือกคำคุณศัพท์"},"topic":{"en":"Words in context","th":"คำศัพท์ตามบริบท"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"The scientist's results were ______: three other teams repeated the experiment and got exactly the same numbers.\"","choices":["reliable","careless","temporary","doubtful"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The clue is that three other teams got the same numbers, so the results can be trusted: \"reliable\". \"Doubtful\" and \"careless\" suggest the opposite, and \"temporary\" (lasting a short time) does not fit.","th":"คำใบ้คือ ทีมอื่นอีกสามทีมทำซ้ำแล้วได้ตัวเลขเท่าเดิม แสดงว่าผลน่าเชื่อถือ คือ \"reliable\" ส่วน \"doubtful\" (น่าสงสัย) และ \"careless\" (สะเพร่า) มีความหมายตรงข้าม และ \"temporary\" (ชั่วคราว) ไม่เข้ากับบริบท"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Three-word phrasal verbs","th":"กริยาวลีสามคำ"},"topic":{"en":"Phrasal verbs and collocations","th":"กริยาวลีและคำที่ใช้คู่กัน"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"I can't ______ the noise from the building site any longer, so I'm going to the library to study.\"","choices":["get on with","put up with","look up to","run out of"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"\"Put up with\" means tolerate or accept something unpleasant, which fits \"any longer\". \"Look up to\" means respect, \"get on with\" means have a good relationship with or continue, and \"run out of\" means have none left.","th":"\"put up with\" แปลว่า ทนต่อสิ่งที่ไม่ชอบ เข้ากับ \"any longer\" (ไม่ไหวอีกต่อไป) ส่วน \"look up to\" แปลว่า ยกย่อง นับถือ \"get on with\" แปลว่า เข้ากันได้ดีหรือทำต่อไป และ \"run out of\" แปลว่า หมด"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Present continuous for actions happening now","th":"present continuous สำหรับเหตุการณ์ที่กำลังเกิดขึ้น"},"topic":{"en":"Verb tenses","th":"กาลของกริยา"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"Look! The children ______ in the river.\"","choices":["swam","swim","have swum","are swimming"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"\"Look!\" shows the action is happening now, so we use the present continuous: \"are swimming\". \"Swim\" is for habits and \"swam\" is past.","th":"\"Look!\" แสดงว่ากำลังเกิดขึ้นขณะพูด จึงใช้ present continuous คือ \"are swimming\" ส่วน \"swim\" ใช้กับกิจวัตร และ \"swam\" เป็นอดีต"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Quantifiers with uncountable nouns in negatives","th":"การใช้คำบอกปริมาณกับนามนับไม่ได้ในประโยคปฏิเสธ"},"topic":{"en":"Sentence structure and agreement","th":"โครงสร้างประโยคและความสอดคล้องของประธานกับกริยา"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"There isn't ______ milk left in the fridge, so we need to buy some.\"","choices":["any","a few","many","some"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"\"Milk\" is uncountable and the sentence is negative, so we use \"any\". \"Many\" and \"a few\" are for countable nouns, and \"some\" is normally used in positive sentences.","th":"\"milk\" เป็นนามนับไม่ได้ และประโยคเป็นปฏิเสธ จึงใช้ \"any\" ส่วน \"many\" และ \"a few\" ใช้กับนามนับได้ และ \"some\" มักใช้ในประโยคบอกเล่า"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Present perfect with since","th":"present perfect กับ since"},"topic":{"en":"Verb tenses","th":"กาลของกริยา"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"My father ______ in the same bank since 2015.\"","choices":["has worked","worked","is working","works"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"\"Since 2015\" shows an action that started in the past and continues now, so we need the present perfect: \"has worked\". The present simple, past simple and present continuous do not go with \"since\".","th":"\"since 2015\" แสดงเหตุการณ์ที่เริ่มในอดีตและยังดำเนินอยู่ จึงใช้ present perfect คือ \"has worked\" ส่วน present simple, past simple และ present continuous ใช้กับ since ไม่ได้"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Superlative adjectives with ever","th":"การใช้ขั้นสูงสุดกับ ever"},"topic":{"en":"Sentence structure and agreement","th":"โครงสร้างประโยคและความสอดคล้องของประธานกับกริยา"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"This is the ______ film I have ever seen.\"","choices":["most excited","excitingest","more exciting","most exciting"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"\"The … I have ever seen\" needs a superlative. Long adjectives use \"most\": \"most exciting\". \"Excited\" describes how a person feels, not the film, and \"excitingest\" is not a word.","th":"\"the … I have ever seen\" ต้องใช้ขั้นสูงสุด คำคุณศัพท์ยาวใช้ \"most\" จึงเป็น \"most exciting\" ส่วน \"excited\" ใช้บอกความรู้สึกของคน ไม่ใช่ภาพยนตร์ และ \"excitingest\" ไม่มีในภาษาอังกฤษ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Subject–verb agreement with neither … nor","th":"ความสอดคล้องของกริยากับ neither … nor"},"topic":{"en":"Sentence structure and agreement","th":"โครงสร้างประโยคและความสอดคล้องของประธานกับกริยา"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"Neither the head teacher nor the students ______ aware that the fire alarm was only a drill.\"","choices":["was","were","is being","has been"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"With \"neither … nor\", the verb agrees with the nearer subject. \"The students\" is plural and the sentence is in the past, so \"were\" is correct.","th":"เมื่อใช้ \"neither … nor\" กริยาต้องสอดคล้องกับประธานตัวที่อยู่ใกล้กว่า คือ \"the students\" ซึ่งเป็นพหูพจน์ และประโยคเป็นอดีต จึงใช้ \"were\""}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"First conditional","th":"ประโยคเงื่อนไขแบบที่ 1"},"topic":{"en":"Conditionals and clauses","th":"ประโยคเงื่อนไขและอนุประโยค"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"If it rains tomorrow, we ______ the picnic.\"","choices":["will cancel","cancelled","would have cancelled","would cancel"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"A real possibility in the future uses the first conditional: if + present simple, will + verb. So \"will cancel\". \"Would cancel\" and \"would have cancelled\" belong to the second and third conditionals.","th":"เหตุการณ์ที่เป็นไปได้ในอนาคตใช้เงื่อนไขแบบที่ 1: if + present simple, will + กริยาช่องที่ 1 จึงเป็น \"will cancel\" ส่วน \"would cancel\" และ \"would have cancelled\" เป็นเงื่อนไขแบบที่ 2 และ 3"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Future passive","th":"passive voice ในอนาคต"},"topic":{"en":"Verb patterns and modals","th":"รูปแบบกริยาและกริยาช่วย"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"The new sports hall ______ by the mayor next Monday.\"","choices":["will open","will be opened","is opening","opened"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The hall does not open itself; the mayor opens it (\"by the mayor\"), and \"next Monday\" is future. So we need the future passive: \"will be opened\".","th":"ห้องกีฬาไม่ได้เปิดเอง แต่ถูกนายกเทศมนตรีเปิด (\"by the mayor\") และ \"next Monday\" เป็นอนาคต จึงใช้ passive ในอนาคตคือ \"will be opened\""}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Past perfect continuous in a time clause","th":"past perfect continuous ในอนุประโยคบอกเวลา"},"topic":{"en":"Verb tenses","th":"กาลของกริยา"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"By the time the rescue team reached the village, the floodwater ______ for nearly six hours.\"","choices":["was risen","had been rising","has been rising","would rise"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The rising continued for six hours up to a moment in the past (\"by the time the team reached\"), so we use the past perfect continuous: \"had been rising\". \"Has been rising\" connects to now, and \"rise\" cannot be passive.","th":"น้ำขึ้นต่อเนื่องหกชั่วโมงจนถึงจุดหนึ่งในอดีต (\"by the time the team reached\") จึงใช้ past perfect continuous คือ \"had been rising\" ส่วน \"has been rising\" เชื่อมกับปัจจุบัน และ \"rise\" ทำเป็น passive ไม่ได้"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Mixed conditional (past cause, present result)","th":"ประโยคเงื่อนไขแบบผสม (เหตุในอดีต ผลในปัจจุบัน)"},"topic":{"en":"Conditionals and clauses","th":"ประโยคเงื่อนไขและอนุประโยค"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"If Ploy hadn't missed the last bus yesterday, she ______ so tired in class this morning.\"","choices":["wouldn't have","hadn't been","won't be","wouldn't be"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"The cause is in the past (yesterday) but the result is now (this morning), so we use a mixed conditional: if + past perfect, would + base verb: \"wouldn't be\".","th":"เหตุอยู่ในอดีต (เมื่อวาน) แต่ผลอยู่ในปัจจุบัน (เช้านี้) จึงใช้เงื่อนไขแบบผสม: if + past perfect, would + กริยาช่องที่ 1 คือ \"wouldn't be\""}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Possessive relative pronoun whose","th":"สรรพนามเชื่อมแสดงความเป็นเจ้าของ whose"},"topic":{"en":"Conditionals and clauses","th":"ประโยคเงื่อนไขและอนุประโยค"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"The marine biologist, ______ research on coral bleaching has been published in three countries, will speak at our school next week.\"","choices":["whose","who","which","that"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The research belongs to the biologist, so we need the possessive relative pronoun \"whose\". \"Who\" cannot be followed directly by a noun here, \"which\" is for things, and \"that\" cannot follow a comma.","th":"งานวิจัยเป็นของนักชีววิทยา จึงต้องใช้ \"whose\" แสดงความเป็นเจ้าของ ส่วน \"who\" ตามด้วยคำนามแบบนี้ไม่ได้ \"which\" ใช้กับสิ่งของ และ \"that\" ใช้หลังเครื่องหมายจุลภาคไม่ได้"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Make + object + base verb","th":"โครงสร้าง make + กรรม + กริยาช่องที่ 1"},"topic":{"en":"Verb patterns and modals","th":"รูปแบบกริยาและกริยาช่วย"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"My mother made me ______ my room before I was allowed to go out.\"","choices":["tidied","tidy","tidying","to tidy"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"After \"make + object\" we use the base verb without \"to\": \"made me tidy\". Thai learners often add \"to\", but \"made me to tidy\" is incorrect.","th":"หลัง \"make + กรรม\" ใช้กริยาช่องที่ 1 ไม่มี to คือ \"made me tidy\" ผู้เรียนมักเติม to แต่ \"made me to tidy\" ผิด"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Inversion after not only","th":"การสลับที่ประธานกับกริยาช่วยหลัง not only"},"topic":{"en":"Sentence structure and agreement","th":"โครงสร้างประโยคและความสอดคล้องของประธานกับกริยา"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"Not only ______ the science competition, but she also raised money for the school library.\"","choices":["won she","she did win","did she win","she won"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"When a sentence begins with \"Not only\", the auxiliary comes before the subject, as in a question: \"did she win\". \"She won\" keeps normal order, and \"won she\" inverts the main verb, which English does not allow.","th":"เมื่อขึ้นต้นประโยคด้วย \"Not only\" ต้องสลับกริยาช่วยไว้หน้าประธานเหมือนประโยคคำถาม คือ \"did she win\" ส่วน \"she won\" เรียงแบบปกติ และ \"won she\" สลับกริยาแท้ ซึ่งภาษาอังกฤษไม่ใช้"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Subject–verb agreement in the present simple","th":"ความสอดคล้องของประธานกับกริยาใน present simple"},"topic":{"en":"Sentence structure and agreement","th":"โครงสร้างประโยคและความสอดคล้องของประธานกับกริยา"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: Every morning my sister [[go]] to school [[by]] bus with [[her]] best friend, who [[lives]] next door.","choices":["go","by","her","lives"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"\"My sister\" is third person singular, so the verb needs -es: \"goes\", not \"go\". \"By bus\", \"her\" and \"lives\" are correct.","th":"\"My sister\" เป็นประธานเอกพจน์บุรุษที่ 3 กริยาต้องเติม -es เป็น \"goes\" ไม่ใช่ \"go\" ส่วน \"by bus\", \"her\" และ \"lives\" ถูกแล้ว"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Past simple vs present perfect","th":"past simple กับ present perfect"},"topic":{"en":"Verb tenses","th":"กาลของกริยา"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: When I [[was]] walking home yesterday, I [[have seen]] an old man [[who]] needed help [[crossing]] the road.","choices":["was","have seen","who","crossing"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"\"Yesterday\" is a finished past time, so the past simple \"saw\" is needed, not the present perfect \"have seen\".","th":"\"yesterday\" เป็นเวลาในอดีตที่จบแล้ว จึงต้องใช้ past simple \"saw\" ไม่ใช่ present perfect \"have seen\""}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Modal verb + base verb","th":"กริยาช่วย + กริยาช่องที่ 1"},"topic":{"en":"Verb patterns and modals","th":"รูปแบบกริยาและกริยาช่วย"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: The children [[were]] so [[excited]] about the trip [[that]] they could not [[slept]] the night before.","choices":["were","excited","that","slept"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"After the modal \"could not\" we use the base verb: \"could not sleep\". \"Slept\" is wrong. \"Excited\" correctly describes how the children felt.","th":"หลังกริยาช่วย \"could not\" ต้องใช้กริยาช่องที่ 1 คือ \"sleep\" ไม่ใช่ \"slept\" ส่วน \"excited\" บอกความรู้สึกของเด็ก ๆ ถูกต้องแล้ว"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"No will in an if-clause","th":"ไม่ใช้ will ในอนุประโยค if"},"topic":{"en":"Conditionals and clauses","th":"ประโยคเงื่อนไขและอนุประโยค"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: You [[will]] pass the test [[easily]] if you [[will study]] hard [[enough]].","choices":["will","easily","will study","enough"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"In a first conditional, the if-clause uses the present simple: \"if you study\", not \"if you will study\". \"Will pass\" in the main clause is correct.","th":"ในเงื่อนไขแบบที่ 1 อนุประโยค if ใช้ present simple คือ \"if you study\" ไม่ใช่ \"if you will study\" ส่วน \"will pass\" ในประโยคหลักถูกแล้ว"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Who vs whom with an inserted clause","th":"การเลือก who หรือ whom เมื่อมีอนุประโยคแทรก"},"topic":{"en":"Conditionals and clauses","th":"ประโยคเงื่อนไขและอนุประโยค"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: The boy [[whom]] I thought [[was]] my cousin [[turned out]] to be a [[complete]] stranger.","choices":["whom","was","turned out","complete"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The pronoun is the subject of \"was\" (\"I thought he was my cousin\"), so it must be \"who\", not \"whom\". \"I thought\" is only an inserted phrase.","th":"สรรพนามนี้เป็นประธานของ \"was\" (I thought he was my cousin) จึงต้องใช้ \"who\" ไม่ใช่ \"whom\" ส่วน \"I thought\" เป็นเพียงวลีแทรก"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Be used to + -ing","th":"be used to + V-ing"},"topic":{"en":"Verb patterns and modals","th":"รูปแบบกริยาและกริยาช่วย"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: She [[is used to]] [[wake]] up early because her family [[has run]] a market stall [[for]] years.","choices":["is used to","wake","has run","for"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"In \"be used to\" (be accustomed to), \"to\" is a preposition, so it is followed by -ing: \"is used to waking\". \"Wake\" is wrong.","th":"ใน \"be used to\" (เคยชินกับ) คำว่า to เป็นบุพบท จึงต้องตามด้วย V-ing คือ \"is used to waking\" ไม่ใช่ \"wake\""}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Choosing a verb that fits the meaning","th":"การเลือกคำกริยาให้เข้ากับความหมาย"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"บทความเติมคำ"},"passage":"Read the passage and choose the best option for each numbered blank.\n\nMangrove forests grow along warm coastlines where rivers meet the sea. Their tangled roots (1) ______ the shore from powerful waves, and the calm water between them provides shelter for young fish, crabs and shrimp. (2) ______ their value, huge areas of mangroves were cleared during the last century to make room for shrimp farms, ports and hotels. When the trees (3) ______, the coast lost its natural wall, and some villages saw their beaches disappear within a few years.\n\nIn recent years, however, many coastal communities have begun to replant them. The work is slow and tiring (4) ______ every seedling has to be pushed into thick mud by hand at low tide. Volunteers often work in (5) ______ heat, and many young plants are washed away by the first big storm. Yet the effort pays off: within about ten years, a replanted area can (6) ______ almost as many fish as a natural forest. Scientists also point out that mangrove soils lock away large amounts of carbon, (7) ______ makes these forests important in the fight against climate change. For a village by the sea, planting mangroves is not just a weekend hobby; it is (8) ______ in its own future.","stem":"Which choice best fits blank (1)?","choices":["promise","produce","provide","protect"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"Roots that stand against powerful waves keep the shore safe: they \"protect the shore from\" waves. \"Protect … from\" is a fixed pattern. \"Produce\", \"provide\" and \"promise\" do not fit the meaning.","th":"รากที่ต้านคลื่นแรงช่วยปกป้องชายฝั่ง ใช้ \"protect the shore from\" (ปกป้อง…จาก) ซึ่งเป็นโครงสร้างคงที่ ส่วน \"produce\" (ผลิต) \"provide\" (จัดหาให้) และ \"promise\" (สัญญา) ไม่เข้ากับความหมาย"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Despite + noun phrase for contrast","th":"การใช้ despite + นามวลีเพื่อแสดงความขัดแย้ง"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"บทความเติมคำ"},"passage":"Read the passage and choose the best option for each numbered blank.\n\nMangrove forests grow along warm coastlines where rivers meet the sea. Their tangled roots (1) ______ the shore from powerful waves, and the calm water between them provides shelter for young fish, crabs and shrimp. (2) ______ their value, huge areas of mangroves were cleared during the last century to make room for shrimp farms, ports and hotels. When the trees (3) ______, the coast lost its natural wall, and some villages saw their beaches disappear within a few years.\n\nIn recent years, however, many coastal communities have begun to replant them. The work is slow and tiring (4) ______ every seedling has to be pushed into thick mud by hand at low tide. Volunteers often work in (5) ______ heat, and many young plants are washed away by the first big storm. Yet the effort pays off: within about ten years, a replanted area can (6) ______ almost as many fish as a natural forest. Scientists also point out that mangrove soils lock away large amounts of carbon, (7) ______ makes these forests important in the fight against climate change. For a village by the sea, planting mangroves is not just a weekend hobby; it is (8) ______ in its own future.","stem":"Which choice best fits blank (2)?","choices":["Instead of","Although","Despite","Because of"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"Mangroves are valuable, yet they were cleared, so we need contrast. Before a noun phrase (\"their value\") we use \"Despite\". \"Although\" needs a full clause, and \"Because of\" gives a reason, not a contrast.","th":"ป่าชายเลนมีคุณค่า แต่กลับถูกถาง จึงต้องใช้คำแสดงความขัดแย้ง หน้านามวลี (\"their value\") ใช้ \"Despite\" ส่วน \"Although\" ต้องตามด้วยประโยคเต็ม และ \"Because of\" ใช้บอกเหตุผล"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Past simple passive","th":"passive voice ในอดีต"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"บทความเติมคำ"},"passage":"Read the passage and choose the best option for each numbered blank.\n\nMangrove forests grow along warm coastlines where rivers meet the sea. Their tangled roots (1) ______ the shore from powerful waves, and the calm water between them provides shelter for young fish, crabs and shrimp. (2) ______ their value, huge areas of mangroves were cleared during the last century to make room for shrimp farms, ports and hotels. When the trees (3) ______, the coast lost its natural wall, and some villages saw their beaches disappear within a few years.\n\nIn recent years, however, many coastal communities have begun to replant them. The work is slow and tiring (4) ______ every seedling has to be pushed into thick mud by hand at low tide. Volunteers often work in (5) ______ heat, and many young plants are washed away by the first big storm. Yet the effort pays off: within about ten years, a replanted area can (6) ______ almost as many fish as a natural forest. Scientists also point out that mangrove soils lock away large amounts of carbon, (7) ______ makes these forests important in the fight against climate change. For a village by the sea, planting mangroves is not just a weekend hobby; it is (8) ______ in its own future.","stem":"Which choice best fits blank (3)?","choices":["were cut down","cut down","were cutting down","have cut down"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The trees did not cut anything; people cut them. This is a finished past event, so the past passive \"were cut down\" is correct.","th":"ต้นไม้ไม่ได้เป็นผู้ตัด แต่ถูกคนตัด และเป็นเหตุการณ์ในอดีต จึงใช้ passive ในอดีตคือ \"were cut down\""}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Conjunction of reason","th":"คำเชื่อมบอกเหตุผล"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"บทความเติมคำ"},"passage":"Read the passage and choose the best option for each numbered blank.\n\nMangrove forests grow along warm coastlines where rivers meet the sea. Their tangled roots (1) ______ the shore from powerful waves, and the calm water between them provides shelter for young fish, crabs and shrimp. (2) ______ their value, huge areas of mangroves were cleared during the last century to make room for shrimp farms, ports and hotels. When the trees (3) ______, the coast lost its natural wall, and some villages saw their beaches disappear within a few years.\n\nIn recent years, however, many coastal communities have begun to replant them. The work is slow and tiring (4) ______ every seedling has to be pushed into thick mud by hand at low tide. Volunteers often work in (5) ______ heat, and many young plants are washed away by the first big storm. Yet the effort pays off: within about ten years, a replanted area can (6) ______ almost as many fish as a natural forest. Scientists also point out that mangrove soils lock away large amounts of carbon, (7) ______ makes these forests important in the fight against climate change. For a village by the sea, planting mangroves is not just a weekend hobby; it is (8) ______ in its own future.","stem":"Which choice best fits blank (4)?","choices":["so that","although","unless","because"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"Pushing every seedling into mud by hand is the reason the work is slow and tiring, so \"because\" fits. \"Unless\" means \"if not\", \"so that\" shows purpose, and \"although\" shows contrast.","th":"การปักกล้าไม้ลงโคลนด้วยมือทีละต้นเป็นเหตุผลที่งานช้าและเหนื่อย จึงใช้ \"because\" ส่วน \"unless\" แปลว่า เว้นแต่ \"so that\" บอกจุดประสงค์ และ \"although\" บอกความขัดแย้ง"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Adjective–noun collocation","th":"คำคุณศัพท์ที่ใช้คู่กับคำนาม"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"บทความเติมคำ"},"passage":"Read the passage and choose the best option for each numbered blank.\n\nMangrove forests grow along warm coastlines where rivers meet the sea. Their tangled roots (1) ______ the shore from powerful waves, and the calm water between them provides shelter for young fish, crabs and shrimp. (2) ______ their value, huge areas of mangroves were cleared during the last century to make room for shrimp farms, ports and hotels. When the trees (3) ______, the coast lost its natural wall, and some villages saw their beaches disappear within a few years.\n\nIn recent years, however, many coastal communities have begun to replant them. The work is slow and tiring (4) ______ every seedling has to be pushed into thick mud by hand at low tide. Volunteers often work in (5) ______ heat, and many young plants are washed away by the first big storm. Yet the effort pays off: within about ten years, a replanted area can (6) ______ almost as many fish as a natural forest. Scientists also point out that mangrove soils lock away large amounts of carbon, (7) ______ makes these forests important in the fight against climate change. For a village by the sea, planting mangroves is not just a weekend hobby; it is (8) ______ in its own future.","stem":"Which choice best fits blank (5)?","choices":["wide","thick","tall","intense"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"Strong heat is described as \"intense heat\". \"Thick\", \"tall\" and \"wide\" describe size or shape and do not go with \"heat\".","th":"ความร้อนจัดใช้ว่า \"intense heat\" ส่วน \"thick\" (หนา) \"tall\" (สูง) และ \"wide\" (กว้าง) ใช้บอกขนาดหรือรูปร่าง ไม่ใช้กับ heat"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Base verb after a modal","th":"กริยาช่องที่ 1 หลังกริยาช่วย"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"บทความเติมคำ"},"passage":"Read the passage and choose the best option for each numbered blank.\n\nMangrove forests grow along warm coastlines where rivers meet the sea. Their tangled roots (1) ______ the shore from powerful waves, and the calm water between them provides shelter for young fish, crabs and shrimp. (2) ______ their value, huge areas of mangroves were cleared during the last century to make room for shrimp farms, ports and hotels. When the trees (3) ______, the coast lost its natural wall, and some villages saw their beaches disappear within a few years.\n\nIn recent years, however, many coastal communities have begun to replant them. The work is slow and tiring (4) ______ every seedling has to be pushed into thick mud by hand at low tide. Volunteers often work in (5) ______ heat, and many young plants are washed away by the first big storm. Yet the effort pays off: within about ten years, a replanted area can (6) ______ almost as many fish as a natural forest. Scientists also point out that mangrove soils lock away large amounts of carbon, (7) ______ makes these forests important in the fight against climate change. For a village by the sea, planting mangroves is not just a weekend hobby; it is (8) ______ in its own future.","stem":"Which choice best fits blank (6)?","choices":["support","supported","supports","supporting"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"After the modal \"can\" we always use the base verb: \"can support\".","th":"หลังกริยาช่วย \"can\" ต้องใช้กริยาช่องที่ 1 เสมอ คือ \"can support\""}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Which referring to a whole clause","th":"which ที่อ้างถึงทั้งประโยคข้างหน้า"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"บทความเติมคำ"},"passage":"Read the passage and choose the best option for each numbered blank.\n\nMangrove forests grow along warm coastlines where rivers meet the sea. Their tangled roots (1) ______ the shore from powerful waves, and the calm water between them provides shelter for young fish, crabs and shrimp. (2) ______ their value, huge areas of mangroves were cleared during the last century to make room for shrimp farms, ports and hotels. When the trees (3) ______, the coast lost its natural wall, and some villages saw their beaches disappear within a few years.\n\nIn recent years, however, many coastal communities have begun to replant them. The work is slow and tiring (4) ______ every seedling has to be pushed into thick mud by hand at low tide. Volunteers often work in (5) ______ heat, and many young plants are washed away by the first big storm. Yet the effort pays off: within about ten years, a replanted area can (6) ______ almost as many fish as a natural forest. Scientists also point out that mangrove soils lock away large amounts of carbon, (7) ______ makes these forests important in the fight against climate change. For a village by the sea, planting mangroves is not just a weekend hobby; it is (8) ______ in its own future.","stem":"Which choice best fits blank (7)?","choices":["that","what","who","which"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"After a comma, \"which\" can refer to the whole idea before it (the fact that the soils lock away carbon). \"That\" cannot follow a comma in this way, \"what\" does not refer back, and \"who\" is for people.","th":"หลังเครื่องหมายจุลภาค \"which\" อ้างถึงใจความทั้งหมดข้างหน้าได้ (การที่ดินเก็บคาร์บอนไว้) ส่วน \"that\" ใช้หลังจุลภาคแบบนี้ไม่ได้ \"what\" ไม่ได้อ้างถึงสิ่งที่กล่าวมาแล้ว และ \"who\" ใช้กับคน"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Noun + preposition collocation","th":"คำนามที่ใช้คู่กับบุพบท"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"บทความเติมคำ"},"passage":"Read the passage and choose the best option for each numbered blank.\n\nMangrove forests grow along warm coastlines where rivers meet the sea. Their tangled roots (1) ______ the shore from powerful waves, and the calm water between them provides shelter for young fish, crabs and shrimp. (2) ______ their value, huge areas of mangroves were cleared during the last century to make room for shrimp farms, ports and hotels. When the trees (3) ______, the coast lost its natural wall, and some villages saw their beaches disappear within a few years.\n\nIn recent years, however, many coastal communities have begun to replant them. The work is slow and tiring (4) ______ every seedling has to be pushed into thick mud by hand at low tide. Volunteers often work in (5) ______ heat, and many young plants are washed away by the first big storm. Yet the effort pays off: within about ten years, a replanted area can (6) ______ almost as many fish as a natural forest. Scientists also point out that mangrove soils lock away large amounts of carbon, (7) ______ makes these forests important in the fight against climate change. For a village by the sea, planting mangroves is not just a weekend hobby; it is (8) ______ in its own future.","stem":"Which choice best fits blank (8)?","choices":["an interest","a payment","an argument","an investment"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"Planting mangroves costs effort now but brings benefits later, which is the idea of \"an investment in\" the future. \"Investment in\" is the natural collocation; \"an interest\", \"a payment\" and \"an argument\" do not fit with \"in its own future\".","th":"การปลูกป่าชายเลนต้องลงแรงตอนนี้แต่ได้ประโยชน์ภายหลัง ตรงกับความหมายของ \"an investment in\" (การลงทุนเพื่อ) อนาคต ซึ่งเป็นคำที่ใช้คู่กันโดยธรรมชาติ ส่วน \"an interest\", \"a payment\" และ \"an argument\" ไม่เข้ากับ \"in its own future\""}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Identifying the purpose of a notice","th":"การระบุจุดประสงค์ของประกาศ"},"topic":{"en":"Notice, email and advert","th":"ประกาศ อีเมล และโฆษณา"},"passage":"Riverside Community Library\nSUMMER READING CHALLENGE: \"Read Around the World\"\n\nWho can join?\nAny student aged 9 to 14 who has a library card. If you do not have one yet, bring a parent and a copy of your house registration to the front desk. Cards are free and take about ten minutes to make.\n\nHow does it work?\nCollect a \"reading passport\" from the children's section. Every time you finish a book set in a different country, write a short review (at least five sentences) and show it to a librarian, who will stamp a new page of your passport. Comics count, but no more than two of them.\n\nPrizes\n• 5 stamps: a bookmark and a certificate\n• 10 stamps: a tote bag designed by local artists\n• 15 stamps or more: your name goes into a draw for a weekend science camp at Khao Yai. The winner may bring one friend.\n\nImportant dates\nThe challenge runs from 1 April to 15 May. Passports must be returned by 5 p.m. on 16 May. The prize-giving ceremony will be held on 22 May in the main hall. Winners of the draw must attend in person; if a winner is absent, a new name will be drawn.\n\nPlease note: the library will be closed from 13 to 15 April for Songkran. Books borrowed during the challenge may be kept for three weeks instead of the usual two.","stem":"What is the main purpose of this notice?","choices":["To tell people that the library will close for Songkran","To advertise a weekend science camp at Khao Yai","To invite young readers to join a reading challenge and explain how it works","To ask local artists to design tote bags for the library"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"Most of the notice explains who can join the Summer Reading Challenge, how to collect stamps and what the prizes are. The camp, the Songkran closure and the tote bags are only details.","th":"ประกาศส่วนใหญ่อธิบายว่าใครเข้าร่วมกิจกรรมได้ สะสมตราประทับอย่างไร และมีรางวัลอะไร จุดประสงค์หลักจึงเป็นการเชิญชวนนักอ่านรุ่นเยาว์ให้เข้าร่วมกิจกรรมการอ่าน ส่วนค่ายวิทยาศาสตร์ การปิดช่วงสงกรานต์ และกระเป๋าผ้าเป็นเพียงรายละเอียด"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Applying a rule from a text","th":"การนำกฎในบทอ่านไปใช้"},"topic":{"en":"Notice, email and advert","th":"ประกาศ อีเมล และโฆษณา"},"passage":"Riverside Community Library\nSUMMER READING CHALLENGE: \"Read Around the World\"\n\nWho can join?\nAny student aged 9 to 14 who has a library card. If you do not have one yet, bring a parent and a copy of your house registration to the front desk. Cards are free and take about ten minutes to make.\n\nHow does it work?\nCollect a \"reading passport\" from the children's section. Every time you finish a book set in a different country, write a short review (at least five sentences) and show it to a librarian, who will stamp a new page of your passport. Comics count, but no more than two of them.\n\nPrizes\n• 5 stamps: a bookmark and a certificate\n• 10 stamps: a tote bag designed by local artists\n• 15 stamps or more: your name goes into a draw for a weekend science camp at Khao Yai. The winner may bring one friend.\n\nImportant dates\nThe challenge runs from 1 April to 15 May. Passports must be returned by 5 p.m. on 16 May. The prize-giving ceremony will be held on 22 May in the main hall. Winners of the draw must attend in person; if a winner is absent, a new name will be drawn.\n\nPlease note: the library will be closed from 13 to 15 April for Songkran. Books borrowed during the challenge may be kept for three weeks instead of the usual two.","stem":"Nok has read three comics and four other books, each set in a different country, and written a good review for every one. How many stamps can she get?","choices":["4","7","5","6"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"Comics count, \"but no more than two of them\". So only two of her three comics count, plus the four other books: 2 + 4 = 6.","th":"การ์ตูนนับได้ \"but no more than two of them\" (ไม่เกินสองเล่ม) จึงนับการ์ตูนได้ 2 เล่มจาก 3 เล่ม รวมกับหนังสืออื่น 4 เล่ม ได้ 2 + 4 = 6 ตราประทับ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Checking details: true statements","th":"การตรวจสอบรายละเอียดว่าข้อความใดถูกต้อง"},"topic":{"en":"Notice, email and advert","th":"ประกาศ อีเมล และโฆษณา"},"passage":"Riverside Community Library\nSUMMER READING CHALLENGE: \"Read Around the World\"\n\nWho can join?\nAny student aged 9 to 14 who has a library card. If you do not have one yet, bring a parent and a copy of your house registration to the front desk. Cards are free and take about ten minutes to make.\n\nHow does it work?\nCollect a \"reading passport\" from the children's section. Every time you finish a book set in a different country, write a short review (at least five sentences) and show it to a librarian, who will stamp a new page of your passport. Comics count, but no more than two of them.\n\nPrizes\n• 5 stamps: a bookmark and a certificate\n• 10 stamps: a tote bag designed by local artists\n• 15 stamps or more: your name goes into a draw for a weekend science camp at Khao Yai. The winner may bring one friend.\n\nImportant dates\nThe challenge runs from 1 April to 15 May. Passports must be returned by 5 p.m. on 16 May. The prize-giving ceremony will be held on 22 May in the main hall. Winners of the draw must attend in person; if a winner is absent, a new name will be drawn.\n\nPlease note: the library will be closed from 13 to 15 April for Songkran. Books borrowed during the challenge may be kept for three weeks instead of the usual two.","stem":"According to the notice, which statement is TRUE?","choices":["Library cards cost a small fee.","The prize-giving ceremony will take place on 16 May.","Students of any age may join the challenge.","Readers can keep books for longer than usual during the challenge."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"The last line says books may be kept \"for three weeks instead of the usual two\". Cards are free, the age limit is 9 to 14, and 16 May is the deadline for passports; the ceremony is on 22 May.","th":"บรรทัดสุดท้ายบอกว่ายืมหนังสือได้ \"three weeks instead of the usual two\" (นานกว่าปกติ) ส่วนบัตรห้องสมุดทำฟรี อายุต้อง 9–14 ปี และ 16 พฤษภาคมเป็นวันส่งพาสปอร์ต พิธีมอบรางวัลคือ 22 พฤษภาคม"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Making an inference from rules","th":"การอนุมานจากเงื่อนไขในประกาศ"},"topic":{"en":"Notice, email and advert","th":"ประกาศ อีเมล และโฆษณา"},"passage":"Riverside Community Library\nSUMMER READING CHALLENGE: \"Read Around the World\"\n\nWho can join?\nAny student aged 9 to 14 who has a library card. If you do not have one yet, bring a parent and a copy of your house registration to the front desk. Cards are free and take about ten minutes to make.\n\nHow does it work?\nCollect a \"reading passport\" from the children's section. Every time you finish a book set in a different country, write a short review (at least five sentences) and show it to a librarian, who will stamp a new page of your passport. Comics count, but no more than two of them.\n\nPrizes\n• 5 stamps: a bookmark and a certificate\n• 10 stamps: a tote bag designed by local artists\n• 15 stamps or more: your name goes into a draw for a weekend science camp at Khao Yai. The winner may bring one friend.\n\nImportant dates\nThe challenge runs from 1 April to 15 May. Passports must be returned by 5 p.m. on 16 May. The prize-giving ceremony will be held on 22 May in the main hall. Winners of the draw must attend in person; if a winner is absent, a new name will be drawn.\n\nPlease note: the library will be closed from 13 to 15 April for Songkran. Books borrowed during the challenge may be kept for three weeks instead of the usual two.","stem":"Napat collected 17 stamps, but he will be on holiday abroad on 22 May. What will most likely happen?","choices":["He will automatically win the science camp because he has the most stamps.","He can send a friend to collect the camp prize for him.","His passport will not be accepted because he has too many stamps.","If his name is drawn for the camp, another name will be picked instead."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"With 15 stamps or more his name goes into a draw, but \"winners of the draw must attend in person; if a winner is absent, a new name will be drawn.\" Having many stamps does not guarantee the prize, and nothing allows a friend to collect it.","th":"นภัสมีตราประทับเกิน 15 จึงมีชื่อเข้าจับรางวัล แต่ประกาศระบุว่าผู้ได้รับรางวัลต้องมาด้วยตนเอง หากไม่มาจะจับชื่อใหม่ การมีตราประทับมากไม่ได้ทำให้ได้รางวัลแน่นอน และไม่มีการกล่าวถึงการให้เพื่อนมารับแทน"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Vocabulary in context: a word with several meanings","th":"คำศัพท์ตามบริบท: คำที่มีหลายความหมาย"},"topic":{"en":"Notice, email and advert","th":"ประกาศ อีเมล และโฆษณา"},"passage":"Riverside Community Library\nSUMMER READING CHALLENGE: \"Read Around the World\"\n\nWho can join?\nAny student aged 9 to 14 who has a library card. If you do not have one yet, bring a parent and a copy of your house registration to the front desk. Cards are free and take about ten minutes to make.\n\nHow does it work?\nCollect a \"reading passport\" from the children's section. Every time you finish a book set in a different country, write a short review (at least five sentences) and show it to a librarian, who will stamp a new page of your passport. Comics count, but no more than two of them.\n\nPrizes\n• 5 stamps: a bookmark and a certificate\n• 10 stamps: a tote bag designed by local artists\n• 15 stamps or more: your name goes into a draw for a weekend science camp at Khao Yai. The winner may bring one friend.\n\nImportant dates\nThe challenge runs from 1 April to 15 May. Passports must be returned by 5 p.m. on 16 May. The prize-giving ceremony will be held on 22 May in the main hall. Winners of the draw must attend in person; if a winner is absent, a new name will be drawn.\n\nPlease note: the library will be closed from 13 to 15 April for Songkran. Books borrowed during the challenge may be kept for three weeks instead of the usual two.","stem":"In the notice, the word \"draw\" in \"your name goes into a draw\" is closest in meaning to","choices":["a picture made with a pencil","a way of choosing a winner by chance","a game that ends with equal scores","a competition for the best review"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"Names \"go into\" the draw and \"a new name will be drawn\" if a winner is absent, so a draw here is a way of picking a winner by chance (like a lucky draw). The other meanings of \"draw\" do not fit.","th":"ชื่อถูกใส่ \"into a draw\" และถ้าผู้ชนะไม่มาจะ \"draw\" ชื่อใหม่ ดังนั้นในที่นี้ draw หมายถึง การจับฉลากเลือกผู้ชนะโดยบังเอิญ ไม่ใช่ภาพวาดหรือการเสมอกัน"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Identifying the main idea","th":"การจับใจความสำคัญ"},"topic":{"en":"Informational article","th":"บทความให้ความรู้"},"passage":"Why Sleep Is Your Best Study Partner\n\nMany students believe that the best way to prepare for an exam is to stay up late and study for as long as possible. Scientists who study the brain say that this plan often backfires.\n\nDuring the day, the brain takes in a huge amount of new information, but much of it is stored only loosely, rather like notes written in pencil. While we sleep, especially during the deep sleep of the first few hours of the night, the brain replays what we learned and moves the most important parts into long-term memory. In other words, sleep turns pencil into ink.\n\nIn one common type of experiment, two groups of volunteers learn the same list of words in the evening. One group then sleeps normally, while the other stays awake all night. When both groups are tested later, the sleepers usually remember far more, even after the tired group has been allowed to rest.\n\nSleep also affects how we feel. Students who sleep less than seven hours tend to find it harder to concentrate, and they are more easily annoyed by small problems. This can create a vicious circle: a tired student works slowly, falls behind, and then stays up even later to catch up.\n\nNone of this means that studying is unimportant. Sleep cannot save information that was never learned in the first place. But a student who studies steadily for a week and sleeps well will almost always do better than one who crams everything into a single sleepless night.","stem":"What is the main idea of the article?","choices":["Sleep helps the brain store what we have learned, so it is an important part of studying.","Students who feel tired are usually lazy.","Students should only study in the morning.","Staying awake all night is the best way to remember new words."],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The article explains that sleep moves new information into long-term memory and that a student who studies steadily and sleeps well does better than one who stays up all night. The other statements are not supported.","th":"บทความอธิบายว่าการนอนช่วยย้ายข้อมูลใหม่ไปเก็บในความจำระยะยาว และนักเรียนที่อ่านสม่ำเสมอและนอนพอจะทำได้ดีกว่าคนที่อดนอน ใจความสำคัญจึงเป็นเรื่องการนอนเป็นส่วนสำคัญของการเรียน ข้อความอื่นไม่มีในบทความ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Interpreting a metaphor","th":"การตีความอุปลักษณ์"},"topic":{"en":"Informational article","th":"บทความให้ความรู้"},"passage":"Why Sleep Is Your Best Study Partner\n\nMany students believe that the best way to prepare for an exam is to stay up late and study for as long as possible. Scientists who study the brain say that this plan often backfires.\n\nDuring the day, the brain takes in a huge amount of new information, but much of it is stored only loosely, rather like notes written in pencil. While we sleep, especially during the deep sleep of the first few hours of the night, the brain replays what we learned and moves the most important parts into long-term memory. In other words, sleep turns pencil into ink.\n\nIn one common type of experiment, two groups of volunteers learn the same list of words in the evening. One group then sleeps normally, while the other stays awake all night. When both groups are tested later, the sleepers usually remember far more, even after the tired group has been allowed to rest.\n\nSleep also affects how we feel. Students who sleep less than seven hours tend to find it harder to concentrate, and they are more easily annoyed by small problems. This can create a vicious circle: a tired student works slowly, falls behind, and then stays up even later to catch up.\n\nNone of this means that studying is unimportant. Sleep cannot save information that was never learned in the first place. But a student who studies steadily for a week and sleeps well will almost always do better than one who crams everything into a single sleepless night.","stem":"What does the writer mean by \"sleep turns pencil into ink\"?","choices":["Notes written at night are easier to read in the morning.","People often dream about writing while they sleep.","Sleep makes weak, new memories stronger and longer-lasting.","Students should write their notes in pen instead of pencil."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"Pencil can be rubbed out, but ink stays. The writer compares loosely stored information to pencil and long-term memory to ink, so sleep makes memories stronger and more permanent.","th":"ดินสอลบได้ แต่หมึกติดทน ผู้เขียนเปรียบข้อมูลที่จำได้แบบหลวม ๆ กับดินสอ และความจำระยะยาวกับหมึก หมายความว่าการนอนทำให้ความจำแข็งแรงและคงทนขึ้น"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Understanding the purpose of a detail","th":"การเข้าใจจุดประสงค์ของรายละเอียด"},"topic":{"en":"Informational article","th":"บทความให้ความรู้"},"passage":"Why Sleep Is Your Best Study Partner\n\nMany students believe that the best way to prepare for an exam is to stay up late and study for as long as possible. Scientists who study the brain say that this plan often backfires.\n\nDuring the day, the brain takes in a huge amount of new information, but much of it is stored only loosely, rather like notes written in pencil. While we sleep, especially during the deep sleep of the first few hours of the night, the brain replays what we learned and moves the most important parts into long-term memory. In other words, sleep turns pencil into ink.\n\nIn one common type of experiment, two groups of volunteers learn the same list of words in the evening. One group then sleeps normally, while the other stays awake all night. When both groups are tested later, the sleepers usually remember far more, even after the tired group has been allowed to rest.\n\nSleep also affects how we feel. Students who sleep less than seven hours tend to find it harder to concentrate, and they are more easily annoyed by small problems. This can create a vicious circle: a tired student works slowly, falls behind, and then stays up even later to catch up.\n\nNone of this means that studying is unimportant. Sleep cannot save information that was never learned in the first place. But a student who studies steadily for a week and sleeps well will almost always do better than one who crams everything into a single sleepless night.","stem":"Why does the writer add \"even after the tired group has been allowed to rest\"?","choices":["To prove that resting during the day is a waste of time","To show that a lost night of sleep cannot simply be made up later","To explain why the volunteers learned the words in the evening","To suggest that the tired group did not try hard in the test"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"Even when the group that stayed awake could rest before the test, they still remembered less. This shows the memory work of that night's sleep was lost and could not be recovered by resting afterwards.","th":"แม้กลุ่มที่อดนอนได้พักก่อนทดสอบแล้ว ก็ยังจำได้น้อยกว่า แสดงว่าการจัดเก็บความจำในคืนนั้นสูญเสียไปแล้ว และไม่สามารถชดเชยได้ด้วยการพักทีหลัง"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Understanding a phrase from its explanation","th":"การเข้าใจวลีจากคำอธิบายในบทอ่าน"},"topic":{"en":"Informational article","th":"บทความให้ความรู้"},"passage":"Why Sleep Is Your Best Study Partner\n\nMany students believe that the best way to prepare for an exam is to stay up late and study for as long as possible. Scientists who study the brain say that this plan often backfires.\n\nDuring the day, the brain takes in a huge amount of new information, but much of it is stored only loosely, rather like notes written in pencil. While we sleep, especially during the deep sleep of the first few hours of the night, the brain replays what we learned and moves the most important parts into long-term memory. In other words, sleep turns pencil into ink.\n\nIn one common type of experiment, two groups of volunteers learn the same list of words in the evening. One group then sleeps normally, while the other stays awake all night. When both groups are tested later, the sleepers usually remember far more, even after the tired group has been allowed to rest.\n\nSleep also affects how we feel. Students who sleep less than seven hours tend to find it harder to concentrate, and they are more easily annoyed by small problems. This can create a vicious circle: a tired student works slowly, falls behind, and then stays up even later to catch up.\n\nNone of this means that studying is unimportant. Sleep cannot save information that was never learned in the first place. But a student who studies steadily for a week and sleeps well will almost always do better than one who crams everything into a single sleepless night.","stem":"In paragraph 4, a \"vicious circle\" is","choices":["a situation in which one problem causes another that makes the first problem worse","a group of students who are easily annoyed","a study timetable that repeats every week","a period of deep sleep early in the night"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The writer explains it after the colon: tiredness makes a student slow, so he falls behind and stays up later, which makes him even more tired. Each problem feeds the next one.","th":"ผู้เขียนอธิบายไว้หลังเครื่องหมายทวิภาค คือ ความเหนื่อยทำให้ทำงานช้า ตามไม่ทัน ต้องนอนดึกขึ้น แล้วก็ยิ่งเหนื่อยกว่าเดิม ปัญหาหนึ่งทำให้อีกปัญหาแย่ลงวนเป็นวงจร"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Inferring the writer's view","th":"การอนุมานความคิดเห็นของผู้เขียน"},"topic":{"en":"Informational article","th":"บทความให้ความรู้"},"passage":"Why Sleep Is Your Best Study Partner\n\nMany students believe that the best way to prepare for an exam is to stay up late and study for as long as possible. Scientists who study the brain say that this plan often backfires.\n\nDuring the day, the brain takes in a huge amount of new information, but much of it is stored only loosely, rather like notes written in pencil. While we sleep, especially during the deep sleep of the first few hours of the night, the brain replays what we learned and moves the most important parts into long-term memory. In other words, sleep turns pencil into ink.\n\nIn one common type of experiment, two groups of volunteers learn the same list of words in the evening. One group then sleeps normally, while the other stays awake all night. When both groups are tested later, the sleepers usually remember far more, even after the tired group has been allowed to rest.\n\nSleep also affects how we feel. Students who sleep less than seven hours tend to find it harder to concentrate, and they are more easily annoyed by small problems. This can create a vicious circle: a tired student works slowly, falls behind, and then stays up even later to catch up.\n\nNone of this means that studying is unimportant. Sleep cannot save information that was never learned in the first place. But a student who studies steadily for a week and sleeps well will almost always do better than one who crams everything into a single sleepless night.","stem":"Which statement would the writer most likely agree with?","choices":["Students should sleep for more than ten hours a night.","Enough sleep can replace studying altogether.","Exams should always be held in the evening.","Good sleep works best together with steady study."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"The last paragraph says \"Sleep cannot save information that was never learned\" but praises steady study plus good sleep. The writer never says sleep replaces study or gives a ten-hour rule.","th":"ย่อหน้าสุดท้ายบอกว่าการนอนช่วยเก็บข้อมูลที่ไม่เคยเรียนไม่ได้ แต่ชื่นชมการอ่านอย่างสม่ำเสมอควบคู่กับการนอนที่ดี ผู้เขียนไม่เคยบอกว่าการนอนแทนการเรียนได้ หรือกำหนดให้นอนเกินสิบชั่วโมง"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Identifying the main idea of a narrative","th":"การจับใจความสำคัญของเรื่องเล่า"},"topic":{"en":"Narrative","th":"เรื่องเล่า"},"passage":"When Mali's grandfather retired as keeper of the lighthouse on Koh Lan Rock, he left behind a cardboard box of notebooks. Nobody in the family had ever opened them. Most people assumed they held nothing but dull lists of dates and ship names, and for the first few pages, Mali thought they were right.\n\nThen, on a page dated March 1979, she found a pencil drawing of a bird she did not recognise: a small grey seabird with a pale ring around its eye. Underneath, her grandfather had written, \"Third year in a row. Always arrives two days after the first strong east wind. Always gone before the rains.\" The same bird appeared again and again over the next twenty years, but the entries grew shorter. In 1994 he counted forty-one birds. In 1998 he counted nine. The last entry simply said, \"None this year. The new jetty?\"\n\nMali took the notebooks to her science teacher, Khun Pranee, who turned the pages in silence for a long time. \"Do you realise how rare this is?\" she said at last. \"Scientists spend fortunes trying to collect records like these, and here are twenty years of them, written by someone who simply paid attention.\"\n\nTogether they contacted a university in Songkhla. A researcher there identified the bird and confirmed that it had once nested on several islands in the gulf but had not been recorded in Thailand for over a decade. The jetty, built in 1996, had brought daily boat traffic and, with it, rats that ate the birds' eggs.\n\nThe story does not end there. Last year the island council agreed to a rat-control programme, and Mali now visits the rock every March with binoculars and a notebook of her own. So far, she has seen nothing. But she writes down the date, the wind and the weather, exactly as her grandfather did, because she has learned that an empty page can be information too.","stem":"What is the passage mainly about?","choices":["How a university researcher discovered a new species of seabird","Why building a jetty is always harmful to island wildlife","How careful records kept by one person turned out to have real scientific value","Why lighthouse keepers in Thailand are no longer needed"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The story follows the grandfather's notebooks from a forgotten box to a university, showing that his careful records were valuable to science. The jetty is one detail, and the bird was not a new species.","th":"เรื่องเล่าติดตามสมุดบันทึกของคุณตาจากกล่องที่ถูกลืมไปจนถึงมหาวิทยาลัย แสดงว่าบันทึกอย่างละเอียดของท่านมีคุณค่าทางวิทยาศาสตร์ เรื่องท่าเรือเป็นเพียงรายละเอียด และนกชนิดนี้ไม่ใช่ชนิดใหม่"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Locating cause and effect","th":"การหาเหตุและผลในบทอ่าน"},"topic":{"en":"Narrative","th":"เรื่องเล่า"},"passage":"When Mali's grandfather retired as keeper of the lighthouse on Koh Lan Rock, he left behind a cardboard box of notebooks. Nobody in the family had ever opened them. Most people assumed they held nothing but dull lists of dates and ship names, and for the first few pages, Mali thought they were right.\n\nThen, on a page dated March 1979, she found a pencil drawing of a bird she did not recognise: a small grey seabird with a pale ring around its eye. Underneath, her grandfather had written, \"Third year in a row. Always arrives two days after the first strong east wind. Always gone before the rains.\" The same bird appeared again and again over the next twenty years, but the entries grew shorter. In 1994 he counted forty-one birds. In 1998 he counted nine. The last entry simply said, \"None this year. The new jetty?\"\n\nMali took the notebooks to her science teacher, Khun Pranee, who turned the pages in silence for a long time. \"Do you realise how rare this is?\" she said at last. \"Scientists spend fortunes trying to collect records like these, and here are twenty years of them, written by someone who simply paid attention.\"\n\nTogether they contacted a university in Songkhla. A researcher there identified the bird and confirmed that it had once nested on several islands in the gulf but had not been recorded in Thailand for over a decade. The jetty, built in 1996, had brought daily boat traffic and, with it, rats that ate the birds' eggs.\n\nThe story does not end there. Last year the island council agreed to a rat-control programme, and Mali now visits the rock every March with binoculars and a notebook of her own. So far, she has seen nothing. But she writes down the date, the wind and the weather, exactly as her grandfather did, because she has learned that an empty page can be information too.","stem":"According to the passage, why did the birds stop coming to Koh Lan Rock?","choices":["Boats using the new jetty brought rats that ate the birds' eggs.","The grandfather frightened them away while counting them.","The rainy season began earlier every year.","The east winds stopped blowing after 1994."],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"Paragraph 4 says the jetty \"had brought daily boat traffic and, with it, rats that ate the birds' eggs\".","th":"ย่อหน้าที่ 4 บอกว่าท่าเรือทำให้มีเรือเข้าออกทุกวัน และนำหนูที่กินไข่นกมาด้วย"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Working out vocabulary from context","th":"การเดาความหมายคำศัพท์จากบริบท"},"topic":{"en":"Narrative","th":"เรื่องเล่า"},"passage":"When Mali's grandfather retired as keeper of the lighthouse on Koh Lan Rock, he left behind a cardboard box of notebooks. Nobody in the family had ever opened them. Most people assumed they held nothing but dull lists of dates and ship names, and for the first few pages, Mali thought they were right.\n\nThen, on a page dated March 1979, she found a pencil drawing of a bird she did not recognise: a small grey seabird with a pale ring around its eye. Underneath, her grandfather had written, \"Third year in a row. Always arrives two days after the first strong east wind. Always gone before the rains.\" The same bird appeared again and again over the next twenty years, but the entries grew shorter. In 1994 he counted forty-one birds. In 1998 he counted nine. The last entry simply said, \"None this year. The new jetty?\"\n\nMali took the notebooks to her science teacher, Khun Pranee, who turned the pages in silence for a long time. \"Do you realise how rare this is?\" she said at last. \"Scientists spend fortunes trying to collect records like these, and here are twenty years of them, written by someone who simply paid attention.\"\n\nTogether they contacted a university in Songkhla. A researcher there identified the bird and confirmed that it had once nested on several islands in the gulf but had not been recorded in Thailand for over a decade. The jetty, built in 1996, had brought daily boat traffic and, with it, rats that ate the birds' eggs.\n\nThe story does not end there. Last year the island council agreed to a rat-control programme, and Mali now visits the rock every March with binoculars and a notebook of her own. So far, she has seen nothing. But she writes down the date, the wind and the weather, exactly as her grandfather did, because she has learned that an empty page can be information too.","stem":"In paragraph 3, the phrase \"spend fortunes\" is closest in meaning to","choices":["spend very large amounts of money","rely on good luck","earn a great deal of money","make guesses about the future"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"A \"fortune\" is a very large amount of money, and to \"spend fortunes\" is to pay a lot. Khun Pranee means scientists pay a lot to get such records. \"Earn\" is the opposite direction, and the fortune-telling meaning does not fit.","th":"\"fortune\" แปลว่า เงินจำนวนมหาศาล \"spend fortunes\" จึงหมายถึงใช้เงินจำนวนมาก ครูปราณีหมายความว่านักวิทยาศาสตร์ต้องลงทุนมากเพื่อให้ได้บันทึกแบบนี้ ไม่ใช่ \"หาเงิน\" หรือ \"ทำนายอนาคต\""}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Inferring a character's thoughts","th":"การอนุมานความคิดของตัวละคร"},"topic":{"en":"Narrative","th":"เรื่องเล่า"},"passage":"When Mali's grandfather retired as keeper of the lighthouse on Koh Lan Rock, he left behind a cardboard box of notebooks. Nobody in the family had ever opened them. Most people assumed they held nothing but dull lists of dates and ship names, and for the first few pages, Mali thought they were right.\n\nThen, on a page dated March 1979, she found a pencil drawing of a bird she did not recognise: a small grey seabird with a pale ring around its eye. Underneath, her grandfather had written, \"Third year in a row. Always arrives two days after the first strong east wind. Always gone before the rains.\" The same bird appeared again and again over the next twenty years, but the entries grew shorter. In 1994 he counted forty-one birds. In 1998 he counted nine. The last entry simply said, \"None this year. The new jetty?\"\n\nMali took the notebooks to her science teacher, Khun Pranee, who turned the pages in silence for a long time. \"Do you realise how rare this is?\" she said at last. \"Scientists spend fortunes trying to collect records like these, and here are twenty years of them, written by someone who simply paid attention.\"\n\nTogether they contacted a university in Songkhla. A researcher there identified the bird and confirmed that it had once nested on several islands in the gulf but had not been recorded in Thailand for over a decade. The jetty, built in 1996, had brought daily boat traffic and, with it, rats that ate the birds' eggs.\n\nThe story does not end there. Last year the island council agreed to a rat-control programme, and Mali now visits the rock every March with binoculars and a notebook of her own. So far, she has seen nothing. But she writes down the date, the wind and the weather, exactly as her grandfather did, because she has learned that an empty page can be information too.","stem":"Why does Khun Pranee most likely turn the pages \"in silence for a long time\"?","choices":["She cannot read the grandfather's handwriting.","She is disappointed that the notebooks contain only ship names.","She is gradually realising how valuable the records are.","She does not believe that Mali's grandfather wrote them."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"Her words afterwards, \"Do you realise how rare this is?\", show that she was thinking about how valuable the notebooks were. Nothing suggests disappointment or doubt.","th":"คำพูดหลังจากนั้นว่า \"Do you realise how rare this is?\" แสดงว่าครูกำลังตระหนักถึงคุณค่าของสมุดบันทึก ไม่มีอะไรบ่งบอกว่าผิดหวังหรือไม่เชื่อ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Interpreting a key sentence","th":"การตีความประโยคสำคัญ"},"topic":{"en":"Narrative","th":"เรื่องเล่า"},"passage":"When Mali's grandfather retired as keeper of the lighthouse on Koh Lan Rock, he left behind a cardboard box of notebooks. Nobody in the family had ever opened them. Most people assumed they held nothing but dull lists of dates and ship names, and for the first few pages, Mali thought they were right.\n\nThen, on a page dated March 1979, she found a pencil drawing of a bird she did not recognise: a small grey seabird with a pale ring around its eye. Underneath, her grandfather had written, \"Third year in a row. Always arrives two days after the first strong east wind. Always gone before the rains.\" The same bird appeared again and again over the next twenty years, but the entries grew shorter. In 1994 he counted forty-one birds. In 1998 he counted nine. The last entry simply said, \"None this year. The new jetty?\"\n\nMali took the notebooks to her science teacher, Khun Pranee, who turned the pages in silence for a long time. \"Do you realise how rare this is?\" she said at last. \"Scientists spend fortunes trying to collect records like these, and here are twenty years of them, written by someone who simply paid attention.\"\n\nTogether they contacted a university in Songkhla. A researcher there identified the bird and confirmed that it had once nested on several islands in the gulf but had not been recorded in Thailand for over a decade. The jetty, built in 1996, had brought daily boat traffic and, with it, rats that ate the birds' eggs.\n\nThe story does not end there. Last year the island council agreed to a rat-control programme, and Mali now visits the rock every March with binoculars and a notebook of her own. So far, she has seen nothing. But she writes down the date, the wind and the weather, exactly as her grandfather did, because she has learned that an empty page can be information too.","stem":"What does Mali mean when she says that \"an empty page can be information too\"?","choices":["Her grandfather's notebooks had many pages that were never used.","Recording that no birds appeared is itself useful data for scientists.","The birds will certainly return if she keeps waiting.","She has nothing to write, so she leaves her notebook blank."],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"Mali still writes the date, wind and weather even when she sees no birds. Her grandfather's \"None this year\" entry helped scientists, so a record of \"no birds\" is also data.","th":"มะลิยังคงจดวันที่ ลม และอากาศ แม้ไม่เห็นนกเลย บันทึกว่า \"None this year\" ของคุณตาเคยช่วยนักวิทยาศาสตร์มาแล้ว การบันทึกว่า \"ไม่พบนก\" จึงเป็นข้อมูลที่มีประโยชน์เช่นกัน"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"gate","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Recognising the writer's attitude","th":"การบอกทัศนคติของผู้เขียน"},"topic":{"en":"Narrative","th":"เรื่องเล่า"},"passage":"When Mali's grandfather retired as keeper of the lighthouse on Koh Lan Rock, he left behind a cardboard box of notebooks. Nobody in the family had ever opened them. Most people assumed they held nothing but dull lists of dates and ship names, and for the first few pages, Mali thought they were right.\n\nThen, on a page dated March 1979, she found a pencil drawing of a bird she did not recognise: a small grey seabird with a pale ring around its eye. Underneath, her grandfather had written, \"Third year in a row. Always arrives two days after the first strong east wind. Always gone before the rains.\" The same bird appeared again and again over the next twenty years, but the entries grew shorter. In 1994 he counted forty-one birds. In 1998 he counted nine. The last entry simply said, \"None this year. The new jetty?\"\n\nMali took the notebooks to her science teacher, Khun Pranee, who turned the pages in silence for a long time. \"Do you realise how rare this is?\" she said at last. \"Scientists spend fortunes trying to collect records like these, and here are twenty years of them, written by someone who simply paid attention.\"\n\nTogether they contacted a university in Songkhla. A researcher there identified the bird and confirmed that it had once nested on several islands in the gulf but had not been recorded in Thailand for over a decade. The jetty, built in 1996, had brought daily boat traffic and, with it, rats that ate the birds' eggs.\n\nThe story does not end there. Last year the island council agreed to a rat-control programme, and Mali now visits the rock every March with binoculars and a notebook of her own. So far, she has seen nothing. But she writes down the date, the wind and the weather, exactly as her grandfather did, because she has learned that an empty page can be information too.","stem":"Which word best describes the writer's attitude toward Mali's grandfather?","choices":["critical","amused","admiring","indifferent"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The writer shows the value of his patient work (\"someone who simply paid attention\") and has Mali copy his method, which shows admiration. There is no sign of criticism, amusement or lack of interest.","th":"ผู้เขียนแสดงคุณค่าของความอดทนและความใส่ใจของคุณตา (\"someone who simply paid attention\") และให้มะลิทำตามวิธีของท่าน แสดงถึงความชื่นชม ไม่มีน้ำเสียงตำหนิ ขบขัน หรือเฉยเมย"}}]};
